    "held_on": "2009-04-30T09:00:00.000Z",
    "question_text": "$C_V$ and $C_p$ denote the molar specific heat capacities of a gas at constant volume and constant pressure, respectively. Then",
    "question_image": null,
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "RQbGT",
        "text": "\n$C_p-C_V$ is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "ruZOl",
        "text": "\n$C_p+C_V$ is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas.\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "Z0fby",
        "text": "\n$\\frac{C_p}{C_V}$ is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas\n",
        "image": null
      },
      {
        "id": "JeNgv",
        "text": "\n$C_p \\cdot C_V$ is larger for a diatomic ideal gas than for a monoatomic ideal gas",
        "image": null
      }
    ],
    "correct_answer": "JeNgv",
    "solution_text": "For monoatomic gas,\n$$\nC_p=\\frac{5}{2} R \\text { and } C_V=\\frac{3}{2} R\n$$\n\nFor diatomic gas,\n$$\nC_p=\\frac{7}{2} R \\text { and } C_V=\\frac{5}{2} R\n$$",
    "solution_image": null,
    "topic": "Degree of Freedom and Specific Heat"
  },
  {
    "question_id": "64b169d9f23ac7d519f9af04",
    "type": "singleCorrect",
